              "text": "**Yes, parathyroid disease can cause neck pain.** While the parathyroid glands themselves are tiny (about the size of a grain of rice) and do not usually hurt, conditions like **hyperparathyroidism** (overactive parathyroid glands) can lead to neck pain through several direct and indirect mechanisms. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Indirect Causes (Bone and Muscle Pain)\nThe most common way parathyroid disease causes neck pain is not from the gland itself, but from how the disease affects your bones and muscles:\n* **Cervical Spine Bone Pain:** Hyperparathyroidism causes the body to produce too much parathyroid hormone (PTH). This hormone pulls calcium out of your bones and dumps it into your bloodstream. Over time, this demineralization can lead to osteoporosis or weakened bones. When this occurs in the vertebrae of your cervical spine (neck), it can cause chronic, aching bone and joint pain in the neck.\n* **Muscle Tension and Spasms:** Calcium is vital for normal muscle and nerve function. The chronic electrolyte imbalances caused by parathyroid disease can lead to muscle fatigue, stiffness, and painful spasms or \"knotting\" in the neck and shoulder muscles.\n\n### 2. Direct Causes (Local Neck Issues)\nIn some cases, the physical gland itself or a growth on it can directly cause localized neck pain:\n* **Spontaneous Hemorrhage (Medical Emergency):** Very rarely, a benign parathyroid tumor (adenoma) can spontaneously rupture or bleed internally. This causes a sudden, severe onset of neck pain, rapid swelling, bruising (ecchymosis) on the neck or chest, and potentially difficulty breathing or swallowing. *This requires immediate emergency medical evaluation.*\n* **Large Adenomas or Cysts:** While parathyroid adenomas are usually small, they can sometimes grow large enough to cause a localized feeling of pressure, discomfort, or a palpable lump in the lower neck. \n* **Parathyroid Cancer:** This is extremely rare, but a malignant parathyroid tumor can present as a firm, sometimes painful neck mass, which may be accompanied by hoarseness if it presses on the nerves controlling the vocal cords.\n\n### 3. Post-Surgical Neck Pain\nIf you have already been diagnosed with parathyroid disease and recently underwent a **parathyroidectomy** (surgery to remove the overactive gland), localized neck pain, throat soreness, and stiffness are very common and expected during the first few weeks of recovery.\n\n---\n\n### When to See a Doctor\nIf you are experiencing persistent neck pain, it is important to have a healthcare professional evaluate it. \n\n* **For routine evaluation:** If your neck pain is chronic and accompanied by other common symptoms of hyperparathyroidism\u2014such as unusual fatigue, \"brain fog,\" joint pain, frequent kidney stones, or high blood pressure\u2014ask your doctor to check your **serum calcium and parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels**. \n* **For urgent evaluation:** Seek emergency medical care immediately if you experience **sudden, severe neck pain** accompanied by rapid neck swelling, difficulty swallowing, or shortness of breath.",
